PRETABLE block -- m88kexcdeliver (#502) TABLE 14, the SER-axis closure.  Written BEFORE any mutant is built.
2026-09-06 ~19:0x.  Corrections go to PRETABLE14_ADDENDUM*.out; this file's mtime is the order proof.

WHY: grok's pass 9 found that v16 corrected the CONTRACT about the serial-mode bit and then closed it at ONE arm.
Its three predicted sweeps were NOT BUILT -- it said so plainly -- so they are measurements owed, and this table
owes them whichever way they land.  v17 (57 rows) is the closure under test.

THE THREE MUTANTS grok named, verbatim in shape:
  M_DIV0SER   `case ILLEGAL_INTEGER_DIVIDE: implemented = !(PSR & M88K_PSR_SER)`
              On v16 (54 rows): PREDICTED SWEEP -- no row faulted at DIV0 with SER set.
              On v17 (57 rows): MUST DIE on `div0tbc', whose PSR_SUPCNOIND now carries SER.
  M_FBNOSER   the fallback `vector < USER_TRAPS_START && !implemented` gains `&& !(PSR & M88K_PSR_SER)`
              On v16: PREDICTED SWEEP -- no fallback row presented SER, though resid50c's blurb claimed the axis.
              On v17: MUST DIE on `resid50ser' (a residual vector with SER set must still HALT).
  M_RRNOSER   the re-route `if (!is_trap)` gains `&& !(PSR & M88K_PSR_SER)`
              On v16: PREDICTED SWEEP -- errind/errc/errpriv/errdiv0 all present SER clear.
              On v17: MUST DIE on `errser', which also carries the MISALIGNED x SER cell.

SCORED ON BOTH INSTRUMENTS.  Each mutant runs against the FROZEN v16 (the state grok reviewed) and against v17.
A sweep on v16 makes grok's prediction a measurement; a kill on v17 makes the closure a measurement.  Neither
number alone says anything -- that is why both are taken, and it is the same discipline tables 12 and 13 used.

POLARITY TWIN, one, because three would be ceremony: CTL_DIV0SERINV `implemented = (PSR & M88K_PSR_SER) != 0`
at the same arm.  SER is CLEAR on every other row, so this must red HEAVILY on both instruments.  It proves the
edit lands and that the arm is reachable; the other two mutants are the same edit shape at sites tables 12 and 13
already proved reachable.

WHAT EACH OUTCOME SETTLES, written before the run:
  * sweeps on v16 AND kills on v17          -> grok right, closure right.  The chain's next pass verifies an
                                               enumeration of 40 cells rather than hunting.
  * a mutant RED on v16                     -> grok's prediction was wrong for that cell and v16 already covered
                                               it; the record says so and the v17 row for it is redundant but
                                               harmless.  NOT a reason to remove the row.
  * a mutant GREEN on v17                   -> the closure FAILED for that cell: a floor breach inside the repair
                                               for a floor breach.  L2, in-round, and the row is mis-cut.
  * the twin GREEN                          -> the edit did not land; nothing else in the table means anything.

CHANNEL per row: div0tbc, resid50ser and errser each already DELIVER or HALT on the fix (measured --
v17_base_on_fix.out, 57/57).  Under its mutant the class becomes un-implemented and the row's outcome changes
(DELIVER -> HALT for the arm rows; HALT -> delivered for the fallback row; and errser additionally loses the
re-route's diagnostic, which REFATAL_EXPECT pins).  Both channels exist and the probe reads both.

APPARATUS: scratch clone /tmp/gxm88kexc_kill, cmp-clean against SEC with no sentinel before the first build; the
frozen instruments are the SHIPPED v17 and the preserved v16; sentinel per mutant, restore by re-copy from SEC +
cmp + REBUILD with `[ binary -nt source ]`; driver run from a frozen copy.  THE DRIVER'S OUTPUT PATHS ARE CHECKED
AGAINST ITS GREP PATHS BEFORE IT RUNS -- table 13's driver wrote one pair of filenames and read another, and only
the denominator caught it.
